  <h1>What Documents Should an IVD Supplier Provide</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> An IVD supplier should provide five documents for a serious purchase: the <strong>IFU</strong> (instructions for use), the <strong>datasheet</strong> (performance specifications), the <strong>COA</strong> (lot-specific test results), the <strong>Declaration of Conformity</strong>, and the <strong>certificates</strong> (ISO 13485, CE, registration). They answer different questions — do not accept one in place of another.</p>

  <h2>The three core documents, and what each answers</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Answers</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>IFU (Instructions for Use)</th><td>How to run the test, interpret, store</td><td>Correct use and interpretation</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Datasheet</th><td>Sensitivity, specificity, LoD, cut-off, specimen</td><td>Performance — the technical spec</td></tr>
        <tr><th>COA (Certificate of Analysis)</th><td>Measured results for your specific lot</td><td>Proves the lot you bought is in spec</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What to request and why</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>IFU</strong> — confirms the intended use, specimen and procedure.</li>
    <li><strong>Datasheet</strong> — the analytical performance (sensitivity, specificity, LoD, cut-off).</li>
    <li><strong>COA</strong> — lot-specific measured values, not a catalogue range.</li>
    <li><strong>Declaration of Conformity</strong> — the CE conformity procedure and standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Certificates</strong> — ISO 13485 and registration, in the supplier's name.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
  <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the document most buyers skip — the lot-specific COA — is the one that actually proves the product they are buying, while the datasheet they do ask for describes the product in general.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> A datasheet is a specification; a COA is a measurement. Both matter, but only the COA ties performance to the specific lot in your hands. A buyer who asks for the datasheet and skips the COA has asked what the product should be, not what it is.</p>
  <p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as an IVD manufacturer's technical team, not a contractual statement.</p>

